# Luminumbra total-audit: byte-hash-gated campaign artifacts.
#
# The campaign is self-attesting: the Codex route-witness shim SHA-256s the routing
# witness into an append-only root receipt, and each task record declares a SHA-256
# for every generated evidence artifact. Those hashes are re-verified post-merge,
# AFTER git has checked the files back out.
#
# This box runs core.autocrlf=true. Without an attribute pin, git rewrites EOLs on
# checkout (LF -> CRLF), so the verified bytes are NOT the hashed bytes and every
# SHA-256 gate fails closed -- no agent can ever pass. It is strictly worse for the
# routing witness, which PowerShell writes with MIXED EOLs (ConvertTo-Json emits a
# CRLF body, the shim appends a bare LF terminator): autocrlf collapses all CRLF to
# LF on commit, then re-expands EVERY LF to CRLF on checkout -- including the
# terminator -- so those bytes can never round-trip at all.
#
# Disable EOL conversion for the campaign tree so committed bytes == hashed bytes.
# This does not weaken any gate; it makes the existing gates verifiable.
.forge/campaigns/luminumbra-total-audit/** -text

# The SAME hazard, one directory over, with a different symptom. Any .forge file that
# git is allowed to smudge (LF -> CRLF on checkout) shows up DIRTY in a freshly created
# agent worktree, and forge attributes that dirt to the AGENT: it failed
# T-LTA-002-census for "modified files outside declared scope:
# .forge/scripts/run-luminumbra-total-audit-wave.ps1" -- a file the agent never opened.
# The diff is empty once CR is stripped: a pure line-ending artifact of the machinery
# commits (which are authored LF into CRLF files). Pin the whole .forge tree so an agent
# worktree checks out byte-identical to HEAD and only the AGENT's real writes show up.
.forge/** -text
